        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Once the TestContext framework loads an "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "ApplicationContext"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " (or "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "WebApplicationContext"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ") for a test, that context is cached and reused for all subsequent tests that declare the same unique context configuration within the same test suite. To understand how caching works, it is important to understand what is meant by “unique” and “test suite.”"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "An "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "ApplicationContext"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " can be uniquely identified by the combination of configuration parameters that is used to load it. Consequently, the unique combination of configuration parameters is used to generate a key under which the context is cached. The TestContext framework uses the following configuration parameters to build the context cache key:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
