      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "For example, if "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "TestClassA"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " specifies "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "{\"app-config.xml\", \"test-config.xml\"}"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " for the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "locations"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " (or "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "value"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ") attribute of "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@ContextConfiguration"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ", the TestContext framework loads the corresponding "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "ApplicationContext"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " and stores it in a "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "static"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " context cache under a key that is based solely on those locations. So, if "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "TestClassB"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " also defines "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "{\"app-config.xml\", \"test-config.xml\"}"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " for its locations (either explicitly or implicitly through inheritance) but does not define "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@WebAppConfiguration"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ", a different "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "ContextLoader"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ", different active profiles, different context initializers, different test property sources, or a different parent context, then the same "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "ApplicationContext"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " is shared by both test classes. This means that the setup cost for loading an application context is incurred only once (per test suite), and subsequent test execution is much faster."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "The Spring TestContext framework stores application contexts in a static cache. This means that the context is literally stored in a "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "static"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " variable. In other words, if tests run in separate processes, the static cache is cleared between each test execution, which effectively disables the caching mechanism."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "To benefit from the caching mechanism, all tests must run within the same process or test suite. This can be achieved by executing all tests as a group within an IDE. Similarly, when executing tests with a build framework such as Ant, Maven, or Gradle, it is important to make sure that the build framework does not fork between tests. For example, if the "
              },
              {
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "forkMode"
                      }
                    ],
                    "kind": "monospace",
                    "roles": []
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "link",
                "target": "https://maven.apache.org/plugins/maven-surefire-plugin/test-mojo.html#forkMode"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " for the Maven Surefire plug-in is set to "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "always"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " or "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "pertest"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": ", the TestContext framework cannot cache application contexts between test classes, and the build process runs significantly more slowly as a result."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          }
        ],
        "context": "admonition",
        "id": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Test suites and forked processes"
          }
        ],
        "type": "note"
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The size of the context cache is bounded with a default maximum size of 32. Whenever the maximum size is reached, a least recently used (LRU) eviction policy is used to evict and close stale contexts. You can configure the maximum size from the command line or a build script by setting a JVM system property named "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "spring.test.context.cache.maxSize"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ". As an alternative, you can set the same property via the "
          },
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "SpringProperties"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              }
            ],
            "kind": "xref",
            "target": "appendix.html#appendix-spring-properties"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " mechanism."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Since having a large number of application contexts loaded within a given test suite can cause the suite to take an unnecessarily long time to run, it is often beneficial to know exactly how many contexts have been loaded and cached. To view the statistics for the underlying context cache, you can set the log level for the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "org.springframework.test.context.cache"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " logging category to "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "DEBUG"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "In the unlikely case that a test corrupts the application context and requires reloading (for example, by modifying a bean definition or the state of an application object), you can annotate your test class or test method with "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@DirtiesContext"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " (see the discussion of "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@DirtiesContext"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " in "
          },
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Spring Testing Annotations"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "xref",
            "target": "testing/annotations/integration-spring/annotation-dirtiescontext.html"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " ). This instructs Spring to remove the context from the cache and rebuild the application context before running the next test that requires the same application context. Note that support for the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@DirtiesContext"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " annotation is provided by the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "DirtiesContextBeforeModesTestExecutionListener"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " and the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "DirtiesContextTestExecutionListener"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ", which are enabled by default."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "When you need to debug a test executed with the Spring TestContext Framework, it can be useful to analyze the console output (that is, output to the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "SYSOUT"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " and "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "SYSERR"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " streams). Some build tools and IDEs are able to associate console output with a given test; however, some console output cannot be easily associated with a given test."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "With regard to console logging triggered by the Spring Framework itself or by components registered in the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "ApplicationContext"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": ", it is important to understand the lifecycle of an "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "ApplicationContext"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " that has been loaded by the Spring TestContext Framework within a test suite."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "The "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "ApplicationContext"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " for a test is typically loaded when an instance of the test class is being prepared — for example, to perform dependency injection into "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "@Autowired"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " fields of the test instance. This means that any console logging triggered during the initialization of the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "ApplicationContext"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
